window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"chem","n":"Chemistry","tab":"Chemistry","w":"Matter and solutions, reactions, and chemistry in daily life.","topics":[{"id":"matter","n":"Matter, Atoms and Solutions","qs":[{"id":"q0276","q":"Which of the following is an example of a colloidal solution commonly found in the kitchen?","o":["Salt dissolved in water","Coffee","Oil and vinegar in salad dressing","Sugar dissolved in water"],"a":1,"x":"DSSSB's key takes brewed coffee as the colloidal solution. Salt or sugar in water is a true solution.","e":"Other kitchen colloids: milk, butter, jelly, mayonnaise.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"An oil-and-vinegar dressing is an emulsion, which is also a kind of colloid; the key still marks coffee."},{"id":"q0731","q":"Sugar has the ability to absorb moisture from the surrounding it is exposed to. One of the following terms that describes this is known as ___________.","o":["Eroding","Condensation","Activation","Hygroscopy"],"a":3,"x":"Hygroscopy is a substance's ability to absorb moisture from the air; sugar and common salt are hygroscopic.","e":"Deliquescent substances absorb so much water that they dissolve, such as calcium chloride.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1748","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Molecular mass of substance is sum of atomic mass of all the atoms in a molecule. II. Number of moles = molar mass/given mass III. Molar mass is number of moles present in 1 gram of substance.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"Molecular mass is the sum of the atomic masses of all atoms in a molecule. Moles = given mass ÷ molar mass (not the reverse), and molar mass is the mass of one mole, so only statement I is right.","e":"Moles = mass ÷ molar mass.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1791","q":"Which of the following statement regarding modern periodic table is correct? I. Atomic radius increases in moving left to right along the period. II. Atomic size decreases down the group.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"In the modern periodic table atomic radius DECREASES from left to right (more nuclear charge) and INCREASES down a group (more shells), so both statements are wrong.","e":"Across a period: smaller; down a group: bigger.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1834","q":"Which of the following was proposed by J.J. Thompson? I. Atom consists of a positively charges sphere. II. Electrons revolve around the positively charged sphere. III. The negative and positive charges are equal in magnitude.","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"J.J. Thomson's 'plum pudding' model: the atom is a positively charged sphere with electrons embedded in it, and positive and negative charges are equal. Electrons revolving around a nucleus came later with Rutherford and Bohr.","e":"Thomson = plum pudding; Rutherford = nucleus; Bohr = orbits.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1985","q":"Which of the following statement is/are correct? I. Isotope of uranium is used in treatment of goiter. II. Isotope of cobalt is used in treatment of cancer.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Radioactive iodine (I-131), not uranium, treats goitre and thyroid disorders; cobalt-60 is used in cancer radiotherapy. So only II is correct.","e":"Iodine → thyroid; cobalt → cancer; carbon-14 → dating.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2031","q":"Which of the following pair is/are correct? I. Isotopes – different mass numbers II. Isobars – different atomic numbers","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Isotopes have the same atomic number but different mass numbers; isobars have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Both pairs are correct.","e":"Isotopes: same Z; isobars: same A.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2073","q":"Which of the following relation is correct? I. Atomic number = Number of protons + Number of electrons II. Mass number = Number of protons + Number of neutrons","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Atomic number = number of protons only (not protons + electrons); mass number = protons + neutrons. So only II is correct.","e":"Z = protons; A = protons + neutrons.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2112","q":"Which of the following statement regarding metals and non-metals is NOT correct? I. Silver is the most ductile metal. II. Gallium and Caesium have high melting points. III. Alkali metals have high densitics.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"All three are wrong: gold (not silver) is the most ductile metal; gallium and caesium have very low melting points (they melt in the hand); alkali metals have low densities.","e":"Gallium melts at about 30°C.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2440","q":"Which of the following elements has 2 electrons in K-shell and 2 electrons in L-shell?","o":["Lithium","Beryllium","Boron","Carbon"],"a":1,"x":"Beryllium (atomic number 4) has the electronic configuration 2, 2: two electrons in the K shell and two in the L shell.","e":"Li 2,1; Be 2,2; B 2,3; C 2,4.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2443","q":"Due to which of the following phenomenon is a plasma created?","o":["High energization of gases","Super-cooling of solids","Ionization of liquids","Super-cooling of liquids"],"a":0,"x":"Plasma, the fourth state of matter, forms when a gas is given so much energy that its atoms are ionised; stars and neon signs contain plasma.","e":"Solid, liquid, gas, plasma (and Bose–Einstein condensate).","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2526","q":"What is the colour of pure acetic acid?","o":["White","Yellow","Green","Colourless"],"a":3,"x":"Pure acetic acid is a colourless liquid; below about 17°C it freezes into ice-like crystals, hence the name glacial acetic acid.","e":"Vinegar is dilute acetic acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2597","q":"Modern periodic table has ______ groups and ______ periods.","o":["16; 7","18; 7","18; 5","16; 5"],"a":1,"x":"The modern periodic table has 18 vertical groups and 7 horizontal periods.","e":"Groups = columns; periods = rows.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1301","q":"Which of the following elements has zero valency?","o":["Lithium","Beryllium","Helium","Fluorine"],"a":2,"x":"Helium, a noble gas, has a full outer shell (duet) and does not normally form bonds, so its valency is zero.","e":"Other noble gases: neon, argon, krypton, xenon, radon.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0908","q":"Which of the following is NOT a colligative property?","o":["Freezing point depression","Relative lowering of vapour pressure","Elevation of freezing point","Boiling point elevation"],"a":2,"x":"Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles: lowering of vapour pressure, elevation of boiling point, depression of freezing point and osmotic pressure. The freezing point falls, not rises.","e":"Salt on icy roads works by depressing the freezing point.","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0935","q":"Which classification method was proposed by J.A.R. Newlands?","o":["Group theory","Periodic table","Law of triads","Law of octaves"],"a":3,"x":"John Newlands (1865) proposed the Law of Octaves: when elements are arranged by atomic mass, every eighth element resembles the first, like musical notes.","e":"Döbereiner's triads (1817) came earlier; Mendeleev's periodic table (1869) later.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0951","q":"Elements with atomic numbers 90 to 103 are classified as _______.","o":["Lanthanoids","Alkali metals","Transition elements","Actinoids"],"a":3,"x":"Elements with atomic numbers 90 (thorium) to 103 (lawrencium) are the actinoids; 58–71 are the lanthanoids. Both are f-block elements.","e":"Lanthanoids 58–71, actinoids 90–103.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1077","q":"The modern Periodic Law is based on ________.","o":["Rutherford Scattering Law","Bohr’s Atomic Law","Octave Law","Moseley's Law"],"a":3,"x":"The modern periodic law (Moseley, 1913): properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic numbers, not atomic masses.","e":"Mendeleev's law was based on atomic mass; Newlands proposed the law of octaves.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1144","q":"A homogeneous mixture of two or more components is a solution. The component present in the largest quantity is generally known as the:","o":["solute","vapour","solvent","precipitate"],"a":2,"x":"In a solution, the component present in the largest amount is the solvent; the smaller components are solutes.","e":"In salt water, water is the solvent and salt the solute.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1175","q":"180 g of glucose (C6H12O6) is dissolved in 1 kg of water. At what temperature will water boil at 1.013 bar (Kb for water is 0.52K kg mol-1)?","o":["373.67 K","373.15 K","373.27 K","373.202 K"],"a":0,"x":"180 g of glucose is 1 mole; in 1 kg of water that is 1 molal. ΔTb = Kb × m = 0.52 × 1 = 0.52 K, so the boiling point = 373.15 + 0.52 = 373.67 K.","e":"Boiling-point elevation is a colligative property: it depends on the number of solute particles.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1188","q":"The concentration of a solution cannot be described by ___________.","o":["parts per million","volume by mass percentage (V/w)","mole fraction","mass by volume percentage (w/V)"],"a":1,"x":"Standard concentration terms include mass %, volume %, mass by volume % (w/V), ppm, mole fraction, molarity and molality. 'Volume by mass %' (V/w) is not used.","e":"ppm is used for very dilute solutions, such as fluoride in water.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1250","q":"In the context of solutions, which of the following is NOT a colligative property?","o":["Depression of freezing point of the solvent","Viscosity of the solution","Elevation of boiling point of the solvent","Osmotic pressure of the solution"],"a":1,"x":"Colligative properties depend on the number of solute particles: lowering of vapour pressure, depression of freezing point, elevation of boiling point and osmotic pressure. Viscosity is not one.","e":"Four colligatives: VP down, FP down, BP up, osmotic pressure.","src":["PRT 3 Apr 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1519","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct about halogens?","o":["This is the only group that contains elements in all three states of matter at room temperature and pressure.","Halogens are group 17th elements.","Chlorine is the most electronegative element.","This is a group of highly reactive non-metals."],"a":2,"x":"The incorrect statement is that chlorine is the most electronegative element. Fluorine is. The others hold: halogens are group 17, highly reactive non-metals, and occur as gas (F₂, Cl₂), liquid (Br₂) and solid (I₂).","e":"Chlorine has the highest electron affinity; fluorine the highest electronegativity.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1537","q":"Which law states that the partial pressure of a gas in the vapour phase is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas in the solution?","o":["Boyle's law","Charles's law","Henry's law","Raoult's law"],"a":2,"x":"Henry's law: the partial pressure of a gas in the vapour phase is proportional to its mole fraction in the solution (p = K_H·x) — gas solubility rises with pressure.","e":"That is why soft drinks are bottled under high CO₂ pressure.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q0869","q":"Which law states that equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules?","o":["Avogadro's law","Law of multiple proportions","Gay-Lussac's law of gaseous volumes","Law of conservation of mass"],"a":0,"x":"Avogadro's law: equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules.","e":"1 mole of any gas at STP occupies 22.4 litres.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]}]},{"id":"reactions","n":"Reactions, Acids and Bases","qs":[{"id":"q0309","q":"Which of the following is an example of a chemical change?","o":["Rusting of iron","Freezing water","Melting of ice","Boiling of water"],"a":0,"x":"Rusting of iron is a chemical change: iron reacts with oxygen and moisture to form a new substance, hydrated iron oxide. Freezing, melting and boiling are physical changes.","e":"Rusting can be prevented by galvanising (zinc coating), painting or oiling.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1606","q":"pH value of neutral water should be-","o":["9","6","7","8"],"a":2,"x":"Pure (neutral) water has a pH of 7 at 25°C.","e":"Below 7 is acidic (lemon juice ~2); above 7 is basic (soap ~10).","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0571","q":"Which among the following acids is also known as 'Muriatic acid'?","o":["Carbonic Acid","Sulphuric Acid","Hydrochloric Acid","Acetic Acid"],"a":2,"x":"Muriatic acid is the old commercial name for hydrochloric acid (HCl); our stomach makes HCl to digest food.","e":"Oil of vitriol: sulphuric acid. Aqua fortis: nitric acid.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1831","q":"Tooth decay starts when the pH of the mouth is lower than ______.","o":["7.2","6.8","5.5","7.8"],"a":2,"x":"Tooth enamel starts to dissolve when the mouth's pH falls below about 5.5, from acids made by bacteria breaking down sugars.","e":"Toothpaste is basic to neutralise the acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2151","q":"By using which of the following strong reducing agents can carboxylic acids be reduced to primary alcohols?","o":["Lithium Aluminium Hydroxide","Palladium","Sodium borohydride","Grignard Reagents"],"a":0,"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH₄) is a strong reducing agent that reduces carboxylic acids to primary alcohols; sodium borohydride is too weak for acids.","e":"LiAlH₄ = strong; NaBH₄ = mild.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"],"k":"Option A is misprinted as 'Lithium Aluminium Hydroxide'; the reagent meant is lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH₄)."},{"id":"q2669","q":"Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct? I. Acid turns blue litmus red. II. Base turns red litmus blue.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"An acid turns blue litmus red and a base turns red litmus blue, so both are correct.","e":"Acid → red; base → blue.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2755","q":"Which among the following acids is not an example of a weak acid?","o":["Acetic Acid","Formic Acid","Carbonic Acid","Nitric Acid"],"a":3,"x":"Nitric acid is a strong acid; acetic, formic and carbonic acids are weak acids.","e":"Strong acids: HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q0926","q":"Who first reported the hydroboration–oxidation reaction in the late 1950s?","o":["Stephanie Louise Kwolek","Hermann Emil Louis Fischer","Herbert Charles Brown","George Charles de Hevesy"],"a":2,"x":"Herbert Charles Brown first reported the hydroboration–oxidation reaction in the late 1950s; he shared the 1979 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for boron chemistry.","e":"Hydroboration gives anti-Markovnikov alcohols from alkenes.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0997","q":"Which natural substance is used to produce litmus?","o":["Algae","Bacteria","Fungi","Lichens"],"a":3,"x":"Litmus is extracted from lichens; it turns red in acids and blue in bases.","e":"Acid turns blue litmus red.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1152","q":"turmeric gives red color when it reacts with _____________","o":["Base","Salt","Water","Acid"],"a":0,"x":"Turmeric is a natural indicator: it turns reddish-brown with a base, such as soap solution, and stays yellow in acid.","e":"Other natural indicators: litmus (from lichens), red cabbage, China rose.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1231","q":"The pH of a 0.01 N acid solution that is 80% ionised will be ______.","o":["0.0269","0.2096","20.09","2.0969"],"a":3,"x":"For a 0.01 N acid that is 80% ionised, [H⁺] = 0.01 × 0.8 = 0.008 M, so pH = −log(8 × 10⁻³) = 3 − 0.903 ≈ 2.097.","e":"pH = −log[H⁺]; a lower pH means a stronger acid.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1233","q":"According to Le Chatelier's principle, which conditions mainly shift the equilibrium of the Haber process towards ammonia formation?","o":["Low temperature and low pressure","Low pressure and high temperature with a catalyst","High pressure and low temperature","Low pressure and high temperature"],"a":2,"x":"N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ is exothermic and reduces the number of gas molecules, so high pressure and low temperature favour ammonia (Le Chatelier). In practice about 450°C, 200 atm and an iron catalyst are used.","e":"A catalyst speeds up the reaction but does not shift the equilibrium.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1510","q":"The letter 'p' in pH stands for ‘potenz’ which in German means __________.","o":["power","proton","potential","percent"],"a":0,"x":"In pH, the 'p' comes from the German 'potenz', meaning power (of hydrogen-ion concentration): pH = −log[H⁺].","e":"pH 7 is neutral; below 7 acidic; above 7 basic.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0864","q":"What is an aqueous solution containing a mixture of a weak acid and its salt, or a weak base and its salt, called?","o":["Hypotonic solution","Unsaturated solution","Buffer solution","Saturated solution"],"a":2,"x":"A buffer solution contains a weak acid and its salt (or a weak base and its salt) and resists changes in pH when a little acid or base is added.","e":"Blood is buffered at pH about 7.4 by the carbonic acid–bicarbonate system.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]}]},{"id":"everyday","n":"Chemistry in Everyday Life","qs":[{"id":"q0036","q":"Select the correct statement with reference to reducing sugars.","o":["All disaccharides are reducing sugars.","Reducing sugars do not react with Tollen's or Fehling's reagent.","Sucrose is a common example of a reducing sugar.","Reducing sugars can reduce mild oxidising agents like Fehling’s solution."],"a":3,"x":"Reducing sugars have a free aldehyde or ketone group, so they reduce mild oxidising agents such as Fehling's and Tollens' reagents. Glucose is one; sucrose is not.","e":"Glucose turns blue Fehling's solution into a brick-red precipitate.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0389","q":"Select the odd one with reference to carbohydrates.","o":["C6H12O6","C12H22O11","C6H12O5","CH3COOH"],"a":3,"x":"C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose), C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (sucrose) and C₆H₁₂O₅ (a deoxy sugar) are carbohydrates. CH₃COOH is acetic acid, a carboxylic acid, not a carbohydrate.","e":"Vinegar is about 5–8% acetic acid.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1943","q":"Which acid is found in spinach?","o":["Oxalic","Tartaric","Formic","Lactic"],"a":0,"x":"Spinach contains oxalic acid. Tamarind has tartaric acid, ant stings formic acid, curd lactic acid.","e":"Spinach → oxalic; tomato → oxalic too.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2441","q":"What is the IUPAC name of Vinegar?","o":["Ethanol","Sodium Chloride","Sodium Bicarbonate","Acetic Acid"],"a":3,"x":"Vinegar is a 5–8% solution of acetic acid in water.","e":"Tamarind: tartaric acid; lemon: citric acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"],"k":"Strictly, vinegar has no IUPAC name of its own; the IUPAC name of acetic acid is ethanoic acid. DSSSB's key means acetic acid."},{"id":"q2487","q":"Which of the following chemicals are present in heads of safety matches?","o":["Red phosphorus and white phosphorus","Antimony trisulphide and potassium chlorate","Potassium chloride and phosphorus trisulphate","Antimony chlorate and red phosphorus"],"a":1,"x":"Safety match heads contain antimony trisulphide and potassium chlorate; the striking surface has powdered glass and red phosphorus.","e":"Red phosphorus is on the box, not the stick.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2525","q":"In which of the following medicines is acetylsalicyclic acid used?","o":["Antacid","Aspirin","Inhalers","Anti-diabetic medicines"],"a":1,"x":"Aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid, a painkiller that reduces fever and inflammation.","e":"Aspirin also thins the blood.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2667","q":"Which acid is found in tomatos?","o":["Acetic","Oxalic","Lactic","Methanoic"],"a":1,"x":"Tomatoes contain oxalic acid (along with citric and malic acids).","e":"Tomato and spinach: oxalic acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1294","q":"Which of the following is present in chalk?","o":["Sodium Hydroxide","Potassium Nitrate","Sodium Chloride","Calcium Carbonate"],"a":3,"x":"Chalk is calcium carbonate (CaCO₃); limestone and marble are also CaCO₃.","e":"Heating CaCO₃ gives quicklime (CaO) and CO₂.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0968","q":"What is the main acid component in vinegar?","o":["Citric acid","Acetic acid","Tannic acid","Nitric acid"],"a":1,"x":"Vinegar is a dilute solution (about 5–8%) of acetic (ethanoic) acid, CH₃COOH.","e":"Lemon has citric acid; tea has tannic acid; curd has lactic acid.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1258","q":"Pyrolusite ore (MnO2) is used for the preparation of _____.","o":["sodium chromate","potassium dichromate","potassium permanganate","sodium manganate"],"a":2,"x":"Pyrolusite (MnO₂) is the main manganese ore and is used to make potassium permanganate (KMnO₄).","e":"Potassium dichromate comes from chromite ore.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1480","q":"Which of the following acids is found in orange?","o":["Citric acid","Oxalic acid","Methanoic acid","Lactic acid"],"a":0,"x":"Oranges and lemons contain citric acid.","e":"Tomato: oxalic acid. Ant sting: methanoic (formic) acid. Curd: lactic acid.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q0839","q":"Which of the following acidic functional groups is present in picric acid?","o":["hydroxyl (-OH)","carboxylic acid (-COOH)","phosphoric acid (-PO4H2)","sulfonic acid (-SO3H) Q The Gandamak treaty was signed in 1879 between which of the following parties?"],"a":0,"x":"Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol) is a phenol: its acidic group is the hydroxyl (–OH), made strongly acidic by three nitro groups.","e":"It was used as an explosive and as a yellow dye.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]}]}]});
